    imageCaption: "Photo by Xinhua",
    image: "/images/article5-xinhua-ukraine.png",

    body: "China — On Saturday, July 11 (Xinhua), 2026, in a statement from the Third Committee of the UN General Assembly, one of the countries opposing the death penalty challenged countries that still maintain the death penalty, including the United States, Iran, and Indonesia, as countries that continue to practice it.\n\nUkraine firmly rejected the death penalty, arguing that executions are not a deterrent or a tool to combat terrorism. \"Killing terrorists creates martyrs and eliminates opportunities for intelligence gathering. A utilitarian approach does not justify the use of the death penalty,\" said the Ukrainian delegate when asked about restorative justice replacing the death penalty.\n\nIran, on the other hand, responded by defending its policy against the death penalty, citing national sovereignty under Article 2, paragraph 7, of the UN Charter. \"No international body has the authority to impose changes on our domestic legal system,\" the Iranian delegate refuted, rejecting the call to impose a moratorium.\n\nIndonesia strongly agreed with this view, emphasizing the importance of cultural and legal diversity. \"Justice is subjective and context-dependent,\" argued the Indonesian delegate, who rejected universal justice. Meanwhile, the United States argued that conflicting academic studies on the deterrent effect justified its use.\n\nHuman rights organizations criticized countries that retain the death penalty for disproportionately applying it to vulnerable groups, including minors and individuals with disabilities. Another option proposed by Ukraine was life imprisonment without the possibility of release.\n\nDebate heated up regarding the second optional protocol to the International Covenant on Civil and Political Rights (ICCPR), which has been ratified by 91 countries. Delegates from countries that retain the death penalty rejected the development of a clear roadmap for the death penalty, viewing it as a threat to cultural imperialism."
