    body: "On 11 July, 2026, as the United Nations Human Rights Council had prolonged and exhaustive discussions on the upcoming 4th cycle of the Universal Periodic Review in Myanmar—often stalled by the delegate of Myanmar's sovereignty-focused stance—the delegates in the councilroom had mostly disregarded substantive solutions on aiding the Rohingya refugees displaced from the rule of the current National Unity Government.\n\nRather, most delegates had discussed legal bureaucracy on multilateral mechanisms and rules of engagement with Myanmar. It was not until the delegate of India motioned to discuss \"refugee issues in Myanmar\" did discussions on humanitarian issues began.\n\nThe Rohingya refugee crisis is not an issue burdened by Myanmar alone; more than 1.3 million Rohingya refugees have spilled over into other neighboring countries, prompting a responsibility to also protect ethnic minority populations outside of Myanmar (UNHCR).\n\nMyanmar has repeatedly vied for the centralization of humanitarian aid as a solution to the refugee crises and cooperation through an \"ASEAN-led platform.\" However, ASEAN member-states' frequent history of rejecting incoming boats of Rohingya refugees and violating the non-refoulement principle has raised significant concerns of the cooperation's feasibility. Furthermore, centralizing humanitarian efforts to the current regime would provide a diplomatic shield for continued exploitation under the guise of state sovereignty.\n\nThe intergovernmentalist approach of the discussed solutions undermine the marginalized grassroots voices in such humanitarian issues, especially in terms of identifying Rohingya civilians between the armed Arakan Army and the investigations crucial for human rights reports. Though the direction of the council is headed towards concessions between Myanmar, it is important to include mechanisms that address the current crises that are straining several countries.\n\n\"It's not a matter about the UPR, it's about the humanitarian crises,\" said the delegate of France in an informal round of discussions."
